### F-008 — Rate limit IP key is trivially spoofable via X-Forwarded-For
### src/lib/rate-limit.ts:76 reads X-Forwarded-For directly from request.
### On localhost / any deployment without a trusted proxy stripping the header,
### an attacker controls the rate-limit key and bypasses ALL caps.

### Test: hammer /api/generate-message rotating XFF
POST http://localhost:3000/api/generate-message HTTP/1.1
X-Forwarded-For: 10.0.0.1
Content-Type: application/json

{"customers":[{"id":"x","name":"a","last_visit":"2024-01-01"}],"template":"standard"}

###
POST http://localhost:3000/api/generate-message HTTP/1.1
X-Forwarded-For: 10.0.0.2
Content-Type: application/json

{"customers":[{"id":"x","name":"a","last_visit":"2024-01-01"}],"template":"standard"}

### Expected: each XFF gets a fresh budget. Vercel edge sets XFF correctly so
### prod is OK, but staging without that header policy = unlimited Claude burn.
### Cost impact: 10 req/s × $0.015/req (Sonnet) = $54/hour attacker spend.

### Defense:
###  - Only trust XFF if the immediate upstream is a known proxy.
###  - On Vercel use req.headers['x-vercel-forwarded-for'] (not spoofable by client).
###  - Add a per-account quota in addition to per-IP.

### Severity: P1 — CVSS 7.5 (cost abuse via rate-limit bypass)
